     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) Project started counselling sessions in Sumgait
2) 25 family legal counseling sessions were conducted in 10 regions
240 family psychological counseling sessions were conducted in 10 regions
3) 520 individual legal counseling sessions were conducted in 10 regions.
484 individual psychological counseling sessions were conducted in 10 regions.
4) 8 legal counseling sessions were conducted in remote communities
7 psychological counseling sessions were conducted in remote communities
In fact, WRC staff in all 10 regions visited 15 remote communities, however not all beneficiaries are willing to received counselling sessions directly during the event at remote community. Thus, most of them turn to WRC office afterwards which is included in number of family and individual sessions.
5) Dr. Aysel Vazirova, international gender consultant, started working on the Assessment in early 2021. Final Assessment of gaps and challenges in community level GBV response has been shared with project, translated into Azerbaijani language. Presentation of the report will be conducted as soon as possible to gather all stakeholders including national partner.
6) Business Development trainings were conducted in 4 regions as follows:
&#x2022;	Feb 21-23, Bilasuvar, 12 women
&#x2022;	Feb 24-26, Sabirabad, 11 women
&#x2022;	Feb 28-Mar 2, Neftchala, 11 women
&#x2022;	Mar 9-11, Salyan, 10 women
Total participants of BD trainings for GBV survivors: 44 women
42 of them successfully submitted Business plans and send for environmental assessment and evaluation process.
As a result 40 GBV survivors received (will received by the end of 2022) in-kind assets to start their businesses.
During the reporting period, the project initiated the business development assistance to women from Salyan, Neftchala, Bilasuvar, Sabirabad. Other 5 regions will be included in business development services later in FY2023.
In February 2022, the project team initiated the preparations for vocational trainings and apprenticeships, which are planned to be conducted in all 9 regions based on needs of beneficiaries. Based on the information gathered from GBV survivors (collected by WRC Coordinators), the project identified the below specializations as most expected by GBV survivors and started headhunting for relevant trainers:
&#x2022;	Nail design
&#x2022;	Hair styling
&#x2022;	Tailoring
&#x2022;	Bakery
&#x2022;	Graphic designer
&#x2022;	Social Media Marketing
&#x2022;	Cashier
&#x2022;	Accounting skills
Starting from April 2022, 235 women GBV survivors in 9 regions are attending vocational trainings on nail design, hair styling, bakery, accounting skills, tailoring skills, computer skills, carpet weaving skills and etc.
160 of them has already successfully completed the trainings and 44 of them has started to earn for life.
7) Dr. Aysel Vazirova, international gender consultant, completed Assessment on GBV and community resilience: connections and challenges. The assessment is under review of the project team and will be presented to stakeholders as soon as possible to gather all of them together including the national partner.
8) The project organized and piloted a new interactive training format as a quest for youth. The &#x201c;Change a story&#x201d; quest drew youth&#x2019;s attention to gender-based violence, and raise awareness among the community members on the proper referral system that will serve to eliminate GBV across the country. This was be a unique opportunity to involve youth in addressing gender-based violence and improve their critical thinking and knowledge on the topic.
Overall 51 youths in Sumgait, Zagatala and Bilasuvar participated in the quest. The main idea was that after half day training youth was empowered to solve a GBV survivor&#x2019;s case based on true story. Teams had an hour to read through the cases, discuss them in details and find out the ways to &#x201c;change a story&#x201d; of GBV victim/survivor. The youth used the knowledge gained during the training for solving the cases.
As a final event, young boys and girls had an excursion meeting at the UN House office for sharing their experience and feedback on the activity. Also, youth were awarded with certificates and present by RR a.i. Peter Batchelor.
9) Besides onsite activities during 16 days of activism, the project also used digital tools to raise awareness and to share success stories. Real GBV stories collected from WRCs across the regions were turned into animations. The animations with short stories were posted on social media and were viewed by more than 60k social media users. 
10) Each WRC in their turn had different events dedicated to 16 days of activism. WRCs in Qusar, Khachmaz, Sabirabad, Neftchala, Masalli, Bilasuvar, Sumqayit, Zagatala, and Baku(Khazar) organized visits to schools, exhibitions, essay and art contests, art therapy for GBV survivors and other community-based events to draw attention to GBV and fight with violence against women and girls. Hundreds of community members participated in these awareness raising events.</narrative>
